🌍 6. ASCII ki problem kya thi?

ASCII mostly English ke liye bana tha.

Lekin duniya mein sirf English nahi hai.

Examples:

ñ
é
ß
ł
č
あ
ب
€

Original ASCII mein in sab ke liye enough space nahi tha.

ASCII ke paas sirf:

128 possible values

the.

Different regions ne different encodings banana shuru kiye.

For example:

    ISO-8859-1
    ISO-8859-2
    Windows-1252
    etc.

Yahan problem start hui.
💥 7. Wrong encoding = gibberish

Imagine sender ne file Encoding A mein save ki.

Recipient ne usko Encoding B ke according read kiya.

Same number ko dono encodings alag character samajh sakti hain.

Result:

Original:
Ø

Wrong decoding:
Ř

Ya kabhi tumhe aise weird characters dikh sakte hain:

Ã©
â€™
� 

Is concept ko generally mojibake / encoding corruption ke context mein dekha jata hai.
Simple rule:

    Jis encoding se data encode hua hai, usko correctly decode karne ke liye compatible encoding information chahiye.

🌐 8. Unicode kyun aaya?

Problem ye thi ki duniya mein bahut saare writing systems hain.

English:

A B C

Arabic:

ا ب ت

Japanese:

あ

Chinese:

龍

Greek:

Ω

Plus:

😊
🔥
♞
€

Ek universal system chahiye tha.

Enter:
🌍 Unicode

Unicode ka basic idea:

    Har character ko ek unique code point assign karo.

Examples:

A   → U+0041
Ω   → U+03A9
あ  → U+3042
ت   → U+062A
♞   → U+265E
😊  → U+1F60A
🔥  → U+1F525

Yahan:

U+0041

ko Unicode code point kehte hain.
⚠️ 9. Unicode aur UTF-8 same nahi hain

Ye bahut important exam/interview point hai.

Beginners aksar confuse hote hain:

    Unicode = UTF-8 ❌

Actually:
Unicode

Batata hai:

    Character ka code point kya hai?

Example:

A → U+0041

UTF-8

Batata hai:

    Us Unicode code point ko bytes mein kaise encode karna hai?

Same Unicode character ko UTF-8, UTF-16 aur UTF-32 different ways mein store kar sakte hain.
📦 10. UTF-8

UTF-8 sabse important hai, especially web/cybersecurity mein.

UTF-8 uses:

    1 to 4 bytes

Character ke according number of bytes change hota hai.
ASCII characters

A

UTF-8 mein 1 byte.

Because ASCII UTF-8 ke andar compatible hai.
Non-ASCII

Example:

Ω

UTF-8 mein 2 bytes.

Emoji jaise:

🔥

UTF-8 mein 4 bytes.

So:

ASCII character → usually 1 byte
Some Unicode → 2/3 bytes
Many emoji → 4 bytes

📦 11. UTF-16

UTF-16 generally:

    2 ya 4 bytes

use karta hai.

Normal/common characters often 2 bytes.

Kuch characters, especially many emoji, 4 bytes lete hain.

Example:

🔥

UTF-16 mein surrogate pair use karta hai:

D83D DD25

Yaani two 16-bit units.
📦 12. UTF-32

UTF-32 sabse simple hai:

    Every Unicode code point = exactly 4 bytes

Example:

A → U+00000041
🔥 → U+0001F525

Advantage: simple.

Disadvantage:

    Space zyada consume karta hai.

Agar A ko sirf 1 byte mein store kiya ja sakta hai, toh UTF-32 mein 4 bytes use ho rahe hain.
🔥 13. Emoji actually kya hai?

Tum screen par dekhte ho:

🔥

Lekin computer ke perspective se ye ek Unicode character/code point ho sakta hai:

U+1F525

Similarly:

😊 → U+1F60A
♞ → U+265E

Computer literally "fire" nahi samajh raha.

Uske paas ek numeric identifier hai.

Phir software/font us code point ko appropriate visual glyph ke form mein display karta hai.
🧠 14. Representation vs Encoding

Room ka ek important conceptual difference:
Representation

Data internally bits/numbers ke form mein exist karta hai.
Encoding

Numbers ko meanings ke saath map karne ka agreed system.

Example:

01000001

Ye simply bits hain.

ASCII ke context mein:

01000001
↓
65 decimal
↓
A

So encoding hume batati hai:

    "In bits/numbers ka meaning kya hai?"

